{"lines":[{"y":0.046511627815394085,"c":0.5,"h":0.008720930389296444,"t":"1718867188","x":0.7716049391467531,"w":0.069958845774332645},{"y":0.053779069625663367,"w":0.057613169352213542,"t":"Test 2","h":0.013081395583944611,"c":1,"x":0.1172839502042468},{"t":"Support for this evolutionary view of behavioral innovation comes from many domains. Consider","x":0.11934156392795425,"y":0.097383720795710005,"c":1,"w":0.77572016398111976,"h":0.014534883982160851},{"w":0.75308639526367183,"x":0.11728397062948082,"y":0.11189634853704133,"t":"one example of an influential innovation in US horseracing. The so-called 'acey-deucy' stirrup","c":1,"h":0.016010627589321258},{"x":0.11522635454824941,"t":"placement, in which the rider's foot in his left stirrup is placed as much as 25 centimeters lower","c":1,"w":0.7613168334960938,"h":0.016195524146054185,"y":0.13074204958358671},{"t":"than the right, is believed to confer important speed advantages when turning on oval tracks. It","w":0.75720163981119792,"x":0.11522634049934481,"y":0.14811542992813964,"h":0.014723203769140158,"c":1},{"h":0.013081395583944722,"w":0.77160491943359377,"x":0.11728395993832161,"y":0.16569767429921367,"t":"was developed by a relatively unknown jockey named Jackie Westrope. Had Westrope conducted","c":1},{"w":0.73045267740885422,"h":0.013083107479890832,"c":1,"x":0.11728394926127521,"t":"methodical investigations or examined extensive film records in a shrewd plan to outrun his","y":0.18168433449619292},{"c":1,"t":"rivals? Had he foreseen the speed advantage that would be conferred by riding acey-deucy? No.","x":0.11522634771050737,"w":0.76543207804361979,"y":0.19758539481462423,"h":0.016195524146054074},{"w":0.75937113444010418,"c":1,"t":"He suffered a leg injury, which left him unable to fully bend his left knee. His modification just","y":0.21353390290979668,"x":0.11722219186550092,"h":0.01748625549747751},{"y":0.23110465101689326,"w":0.74279836018880219,"x":0.11522633420572476,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"happened to coincide with enhanced left-hand turning performance. This led to the rapid and","c":1},{"x":0.11522634425354795,"y":0.24705535915241739,"h":0.016026036337772775,"w":0.73251027425130211,"c":1,"t":"widespread adoption of riding acey-deucy by many riders, a racing style which continues in"},{"y":0.26442873985373638,"t":"today's thoroughbred racing.","w":0.23045267740885417,"x":0.115226336062006,"h":0.014723203769140158,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.29796511613310239,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"Plenty of other stories show that fresh advances can arise from error, misadventure, and also","w":0.73662551879882809,"x":0.11522633514584754},{"x":0.11522633704381575,"c":1,"t":"pure serendipity - a happy accident. For example, in the early 1970s, two employees of the","y":0.31389870418565602,"h":0.016043156420524562,"w":0.72427983601888013},{"w":0.76954732259114589,"y":0.32979976453051285,"t":"company 3M each had a problem: Spencer Silver had a product - a glue which was only slightly","c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185,"x":0.11522633862534197},{"x":0.11522633797361266,"y":0.34738372078418367,"w":0.71810699462890626,"c":1,"t":"sticky - and no use for it, while his colleague Art Fry was trying to figure out how to affix","h":0.01598837238037687},{"c":1,"t":"temporary bookmarks in his hymn book without damaging its pages. The solution to both these","x":0.11522633956352138,"y":0.36336866908378729,"w":0.76337448120117191,"h":0.015991796172268979},{"w":0.74691360473632806,"x":0.11728393826589732,"y":0.38074204923262112,"h":0.014723203769140158,"t":"problems was the invention of the brilliantly simple yet phenomenally successful Post-It note.","c":1},{"t":"Such examples give lie to the claim that ingenious, designing minds are responsible for human","y":0.3968023254486488,"x":0.11728394548429605,"w":0.75514404296874993,"h":0.014563987898180697,"c":1},{"y":0.41170231466228313,"t":"creativity and invention. Far more banal and mechanical forces may be at work; forces that are","x":0.1151641101588721,"w":0.75322240193684897,"h":0.019221673163424047,"c":1},{"w":0.38683128356933588,"h":0.014555427856804748,"x":0.11522633340869808,"y":0.43021201412989774,"c":1,"t":"fundamentally connected to the laws of science."},{"y":0.46366279055177073,"h":0.016019188192342826,"c":1,"t":"The notions of insight, creativity and genius are often invoked, but they remain vague and of","w":0.73868311564127609,"x":0.11728395647977341},{"w":0.78395060221354185,"t":"doubtful scientific utility, especially when one considers the diverse and enduring contributions of","y":0.47819767460548879,"x":0.11522634140866131,"h":0.017441859655301539,"c":1},{"y":0.49705535915253907,"h":0.016026036337772664,"t":"individuals such as Plato, Leonardo da Vinci, Shakespeare, Beethoven, Galileo, Newton, Kepler,","x":0.1152263467618504,"w":0.77160491943359377,"c":1},{"x":0.11934155582721483,"w":0.77366256713867176,"h":0.014723203769140158,"c":1,"y":0.5129564195106957,"t":"Curie, Pasteur and Edison. These notions merely label rather than explain the evolution of human"},{"w":0.60493825276692703,"t":"innovations. We need another approach, and there is a promising candidate.","x":0.11522634674205942,"c":1,"y":0.52906976728150357,"h":0.017455556507806946},{"h":0.016195524146054185,"x":0.11728394014636991,"y":0.56242638383506971,"c":1,"t":"The Law of Effect was advanced by psychologist Edward Thorndike in 1898, some 40 years","w":0.73456792195638021},{"w":0.76759485880533862,"y":0.57831611491036627,"x":0.11517639602871914,"t":"after Charles Darwin published his groundbreaking work on biological evolution, On the Origin","h":0.019014537123824948,"c":1},{"t":"of Species. This simple law holds that organisms tend to repeat successful behaviors and to","w":0.72427983601888024,"c":1,"h":0.01598837238037687,"y":0.59593023241186727,"x":0.11522633702102315},{"y":0.61189634873823584,"x":0.11728396030012865,"h":0.014557139752750858,"c":1,"t":"refrain from performing unsuccessful ones. Just like Darwin's Law of Natural Selection, the Law","w":0.77160491943359377},{"w":0.7366255187988282,"c":1,"y":0.62926972886873356,"x":0.11522633514966467,"t":"of Effect involves an entirely mechanical process of variation and selection, without any end","h":0.016195524146054185},{"y":0.64371986061399744,"w":0.1467843882242838,"h":0.01806150982320931,"t":"objective in sight.","c":1,"x":0.11493851858129349},{"t":"Of course, the origin of human innovation demands much further study. In particular, the","y":0.67873969376801524,"w":0.70987655639648439,"h":0.016027748233718775,"x":0.11728394392140919,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.016195524146054185,"y":0.69464075354781929,"t":"provenance of the raw material on which the Law of Effect operates is not as clearly known as","x":0.11522634155375319,"w":0.75102879842122383},{"c":1,"t":"that of the genetic mutations on which the Law of Natural Selection operates. The generation of","w":0.76748972574869789,"x":0.11522633044516899,"h":0.01453488398216074,"y":0.7122093021928898},{"t":"novel ideas and behaviors may not be entirely random, but constrained by prior successes and","x":0.11515192619921512,"y":0.72626672085004229,"c":1,"h":0.019940222390829709,"w":0.74913040161132816},{"t":"failures - of the current individual (such as Bohr) or of predecessors (such as Nicholson).","x":0.11522633799872911,"y":0.74411071843600363,"w":0.71810699462890626,"h":0.0175171886933847,"c":1},{"y":0.7776162789100729,"t":"The time seems right for abandoning the naive notions of intelligent design and genius, and for","x":0.11728396183673993,"w":0.7592592366536457,"h":0.017441860778592999,"c":1},{"t":"scientifically exploring the true origins of creative behavior.","y":0.79505300369939158,"h":0.015993508068214979,"x":0.11522634017701099,"c":1,"w":0.48148147583007811},{"c":1,"t":"46","w":0.026748971939086916,"y":0.86773255796252025,"x":0.11522633697284339,"h":0.013081395583944722},{"y":0.95318838622914936,"t":"IELTSXpress.com","x":0.66992475471958191,"w":0.30618598937988273,"h":0.03366544698798335,"c":1}],"h":3395,"w":2400}